# fix symmetry example: 8 LJ atoms with inversion symmetry (space group P-1)
#
# Four atoms are placed in one half of the box and their images under
# inversion through the origin of the box in the other half.  Without
# perturbations, plain MD would keep this symmetry, since the forces
# are symmetric.  But tiny deviations from perfect symmetry, e.g. from
# round-off errors, grow exponentially and destroy the symmetry.
#
# The first run with fix symmetry keeps the symmetry exactly.  For the
# second run, the fix is removed and one atom is displaced by 1.0e-4.
# The deviation from symmetry, shown in the last thermo column, grows by
# five orders of magnitude within a few thousand steps.  Round-off errors
# of about 1.0e-16 have the same effect, only it takes longer and the
# result depends on the platform and the number of MPI processes.

units           lj
atom_style      atomic
boundary        p p p

region          box block 0 8 0 8 0 8
create_box      1 box
